abstract:
Abstract Degradations of machined surface integrity due to thermal softening and strain hardening are prevalently found in machining of titanium alloys due to their low thermal conductivity (~ 6.7 W/m·K). However, these contradictory effects can alternatively occur due to interactions in cutting environments and tool wear patterns. This study investigates the influences of cutting environments on the contradictory physical effects of thermal softening and strain hardening in cutting Ti-6Al-4 V parts under dry, flooding, subcooled and cryogenic conditions. By inversely correlating the cutting forces against the cutting lengths, tool wear and cutting environments, the altered cutting force coefficients ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
